Another alternative to inspecting the `RetryException` is to use your own custom `StopStrategy` implementation when building a `Retryer`. You can have it behave just like `stopAfterAttempt()` and then run some custom logic that you want to occur on stopping, such as something like this example:

``` java
interface OnStop {
    void execute();
}

public class CustomStopStrategy implements StopStrategy {

    private final int maxAttemptNumber;
    private final OnStop onStop;

    public CustomStopStrategy(int maxAttemptNumber, OnStop onStop) {
        this.maxAttemptNumber = maxAttemptNumber;
        this.onStop = onStop;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean shouldStop(int previousAttemptNumber, long delaySinceFirstAttemptInMillis) {
        boolean shouldStop = previousAttemptNumber >= maxAttemptNumber;
        if(shouldStop) {
            // just run this if we're about to stop
            onStop.execute();
        }
        return shouldStop;
    }
}
```

Then you just set it with:

``` java
withStopStrategy(new CustomStopStrategy(3, someOnStopImplementation))
```

Care should be taken to manage any state and thread safety in your custom logic as well as how it might also handle additional exceptions that could be introduced since they can escape from the normal retry `call()` flow.

I added the interface/callback in here in case you wanted to generalize this mechanism a bit more, but you could just as easily get rid of that and put your DB code directly in the `if` block above.

@marcomorain thanks for a suggestion, but no matter how hard I tried I couldn't get it working the way you describe.

What I did instead is I left callable as is, but reversed result predicate, so instead of using `retryIfResult(not(equalTo(expected)))` I use `retryIfResult(equalTo(expected))`. This causes retry on success. Then if `retryer.call(operation)` returns successfully it means there was an error. Also if it throws `RetryException` I catch it and do nothing as it means that all attempts were successful.

It is cumbersome, but works with current retryer API.

